course: dictionary
lesson: 15833
title: Saying less than it really is
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: To understate something means to say it is smaller, less important or less serious than it really is. «The storm was a bit breezy, really,» said Sam, when the roof had already blown off. That is an understatement, because the storm was far worse than breezy.
explain: Check it by asking: is the speaker saying less than the truth? If yes, they are understating it. Look at the clues around the word: a roof blown off, a flooded road and a ruined picnic all show the real size of the problem.
explain: Understated also describes a style. «She wore an understated grey dress to the party» means the dress was simple and quiet, not showy. So understate can be a verb about how we talk, and understated can be an adjective about how something looks.
fact: To understate means to say something is smaller or less serious than it really is.
fact: «Understate» is a verb: understate, understates, understated, understating.
fact: «Understated» can also be an adjective meaning simple and quiet, not showy.
fact: An understatement is saying less than you mean, often to be funny, as in «It's a bit wet» during a flood.
fact: Understating danger or numbers in the news can mislead people, so always check the real size of a problem.
exercise: «The coach understated how hard the race would be. It was the hardest race of her life.» What did the coach do? | Won the race easily | Ran the race for her | Made the race much harder than it was | *Said the race was less hard than it really was
exercise: «She wore an understated grey dress to the party.» What does understated mean here? | Torn and dirty from the rain | *Simple and quiet, not showy | Loud and bright, with lots of sparkles | Very long and expensive
exercise: What is the best meaning of to understate? | *To say something is smaller or less serious than it really is | To shout very loudly | To say something is bigger than it really is | To copy something exactly
test: «The storm tore the roof off, but Mia said, "It was a little breeze."» What is Mia doing? | *Understating how bad the storm was | Telling the exact truth | Asking a question | Making the storm bigger
test: Which sentence is an understatement? | *«It's a tiny bit wet» said in a flood | «The mountain is very tall.» | «The sun is shining brightly.» | «I am very cold, in a freezing snowstorm.»
exam: Why can understating danger in the news be a problem? | It is always funny | It makes the danger grow bigger | *It can mislead people about how serious the danger really is | It makes the news longer
